<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      Phase 13-08 (D-17): the forced-change flow needs a single-use, hashed, short-lived token, and
      password_reset_tokens already models exactly that — a hash, an expiry and a used-at stamp,
      behind one row-level-security policy.

      WHY THIS TABLE RATHER THAN A SECOND ONE. A credential-bearing token is a thing you want in as
      few places as possible. One table means one RLS policy to get right, one grant to auth_user,
      one expiry sweep to write when someone finally writes it, and one place to look when a token
      leaks. Two tables means two of each, and the second one drifts — which is the failure mode
      this whole phase's audit is about. The cost is a discriminator column, which is cheap and,
      unlike a second table, is enforced by the database.

      NAMING NOTE: changeset "auth-1.0.0-061-enable-rls-password-reset-tokens" already lives INSIDE
      060-create-password-reset-tokens.xml. Liquibase keys on (id, author, filename) so there is no
      collision, but the ids here are deliberately suffixed differently so a human reading
      DATABASECHANGELOG can tell the two apart.
    -->
    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-061-password-token-purpose-column" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <columnExists tableName="password_reset_tokens" columnName="purpose"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>
        <!--
          defaultValue RESET, not NULL-then-backfill: every row that exists when this runs was
          written by PasswordResetService, so RESET is the true value for all of them and the
          default also makes the column safe for any in-flight insert from a not-yet-restarted
          instance. A NOT NULL column with a default is one statement in Postgres 11+; no rewrite.
        -->
        <addColumn tableName="password_reset_tokens">
            <column name="purpose" type="VARCHAR(20)" defaultValue="RESET">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </addColumn>
        <!--
          The check constraint is the point of the column. Without it "purpose" is a free-text field
          and a typo ('FORCED-CHANGE', 'forced_change') produces a token that can never be redeemed
          by anything — an outage that looks like a token-generation bug. With it, the typo fails at
          the INSERT, in the service that made it, with the offending value in the message.
        -->
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            ALTER TABLE password_reset_tokens
                ADD CONSTRAINT ck_password_reset_tokens_purpose
                CHECK (purpose IN ('RESET', 'FORCED_CHANGE'));
        </sql>
        <!--
          Redemption always matches hash AND purpose together (a reset token presented at the
          forced-change endpoint must miss, and vice versa), so the index carries both. The existing
          hash-only index stays: it is what an operator greps with, and dropping it would be a
          behaviour change to the reset path for no gain.
        -->
        <createIndex tableName="password_reset_tokens" indexName="idx_password_reset_tokens_hash_purpose">
            <column name="token_hash"/>
            <column name="purpose"/>
        </createIndex>
        <rollback>
            <dropIndex tableName="password_reset_tokens" indexName="idx_password_reset_tokens_hash_purpose"/>
            <sql splitStatements="false">
                ALTER TABLE password_reset_tokens DROP CONSTRAINT IF EXISTS ck_password_reset_tokens_purpose;
            </sql>
            <dropColumn tableName="password_reset_tokens" columnName="purpose"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
      THE PART WITHOUT WHICH NONE OF THIS WORKS, and which was already broken before this plan.

      password_reset_tokens is FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY on app.current_tenant_id. Both flows that
      redeem a token by hash — forgot-password confirm, and now forced change — are PUBLIC: the
      caller has no JWT, so JwtAuthenticationFilter never populates TenantContext and
      TenantAwareDataSource sets no GUC. The policy therefore hides the row from the very lookup
      that has to find it.

      Measured on the live dev auth_db (auth_user is NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS), on this branch,
      before this changeset: a freshly issued, unused, unexpired reset token was refused with
      "Invalid or expired reset token" while the matching row was demonstrably present, unused and
      unexpired. The forgot-password flow has never worked against a database that enforces RLS.
      Every integration test passed regardless, because Testcontainers' Postgres user is a
      SUPERUSER and superusers bypass row security entirely — the same blind spot that let 13-02's
      and 13-06's write paths ship broken.

      The fix is the idiom this codebase already uses for exactly this situation: changeset 052's
      auth_lookup_refresh_tenant, which resolves a refresh session's tenant before the GUC can be
      set. SECURITY DEFINER, so it runs as the function owner and sees past the policy; STABLE and
      parameterised, so it cannot be used for anything else; EXECUTE granted to auth_user only.

      It is not an oracle. It answers only for a caller who already presents the exact SHA-256 of a
      256-bit token, and it returns a tenant id and nothing else — no user, no email, no validity.
      Whether the token is usable is decided afterwards, by a conditional UPDATE, under the policy.
    -->
    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-061-password-token-lookup-function" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION auth_lookup_password_token_tenant(p_token_hash TEXT, p_purpose TEXT)
            RETURNS UUID
            LANGUAGE sql
            SECURITY DEFINER
            SET search_path = public
            STABLE
            AS $body$
              SELECT tenant_id FROM password_reset_tokens
               WHERE token_hash = p_token_hash AND purpose = p_purpose
               LIMIT 1;
            $body$;

            REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION auth_lookup_password_token_tenant(TEXT, TEXT) FROM PUBLIC;
            GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION auth_lookup_password_token_tenant(TEXT, TEXT) TO auth_user;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="false">
                REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION auth_lookup_password_token_tenant(TEXT, TEXT) FROM auth_user;
                DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS auth_lookup_password_token_tenant(TEXT, TEXT);
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
